# docs/process.toml — HOW ITEMS ARE PROCESSED. Hand-edited, machine-read.
#
# One home for every POLICY DIAL (SN-028). Before this file the dials lived in
# ~10 one-word files under docs/ (docs/gate-policy, docs/push-policy, …). That
# idiom was never a ruling — it accreted, and the one constraint that really
# held it in place was the git hooks' pure-sh parse (repo-review M-42,
# 2026-07-21: a Python-less box must still FAIL CLOSED on a declared privacy
# policy). A one-word-per-file format is the only thing a `head -n 1` parse can
# read; the old 3.8 floor's lack of `tomllib` did the rest.
#
# Both reasons are spent. The floor is 3.11 (`tomllib` is stdlib), and the
# hooks now do a KEYED match instead of a first-line one — asymmetrically, so
# the two grammars cannot disagree in the dangerous direction:
#
#     declared?  any `privacy_check =` on a non-comment line  (BROAD)
#     off?       only a value provably `false`                 (NARROW)
#     otherwise  ON — fail closed
#
# which is why every value below is written ONE `key = value` PER LINE, under a
# bare [section] header, with no dotted keys, no inline tables and no
# multi-line strings. That shape is CHECKED, not merely conventional
# (`agent_common.process_shape_findings` refuses anything else), and
# tests/test_process_config.py drives the shipped hooks' sh against `tomllib`
# over a table of adversarial files — because a claim of agreement that no test
# drives is exactly how two parsers diverge.
#
# WHAT IS IN SCOPE: every dial that answers "how is work PROCESSED" — gate
# authority, the human-ratification level, push authority, the reviewer count,
# the privacy and secrets gates, guardrails, the blackout window — and, since
# the 2026-08-11 overturn below, every dial that answers "is this CHECK on".
# What stays outside, and why:
#
#   1. `docs/stack.ini` — the PRODUCT TOOLCHAIN, not a process dial. It is
#      adopter-owned across re-syncs (ADOPTING.md "Preserve always"), has five
#      Python consumers and a working format. Folding it in here would put an
#      adopter-owned file under a kit-owned template.
#   2. PRESENCE-AS-SEMANTICS files stay files: `docs/work/pause` (delete-to-
#      resume IS the contract) and `docs/agents-enabled` (presence = consent to
#      managed routing). A key in a shared file cannot express deletion-as-an-
#      act, nor survive as a one-line reviewed diff.
#   3. `docs/gate` — a GENERATED CACHE (derive_gate.py), not configuration.
#   (There used to be a fourth exception here — the six CHECK-ENABLEMENT
#   toggles stay one-word files, WI-423. It is OVERTURNED, owner 2026-08-11:
#   "creating files to toggle something off is also very confusing … far
#   better to tie those into process.toml and key them all to on / true."
#   They are now the [checks] section at the bottom of this file. The ruling
#   answers the objection rather than ignoring it: shipping six visible keys
#   IS the point, because absence-as-declaration is unreadable — a repo that
#   declares nothing looks identical to one that never heard of the layer.
#   The cost the old text priced is real and is PAID, not hidden: the hooks
#   never sh-parse these six, so folding them replicates no greppable
#   contract, and each independently-copyable checker grows its own small
#   `tomllib` read (F5 — no shared `_kitcommon.py`, owner 2026-07-12). Every
#   checker also keeps reading its legacy one-word file as a MIGRATION-WINDOW
#   fallback, the way check_privacy.py reads `docs/privacy-check` — see the
#   [checks] header for the expiry.)
#
# MIGRATION: a repo that still carries the legacy one-word files is converted
# automatically by `python scripts/bootstrap.py --migrate-config --dest .`
# (bootstrap and the downstream re-sync run it for you). Running with BOTH
# sources live is REFUSED, not silently resolved — a mixed config is exactly
# the state where two readers disagree about the same policy.

[attestation]
# THE GATE-AUTHORITY DIAL. This section used to open with a three-value enum
# (`gate_policy = "attended" | "single-ratify" | "autonomous"`). SN-029 RETIRED
# it and this file no longer ships the key, because shipping both was itself
# the bug: the template's `human_ratification_through = 4` shadowed whatever
# word `bootstrap --gate-policy` had written, so a repo that chose `autonomous`
# scaffolded as fully attended with no diagnostic anywhere.
#
# The three words map onto the dials below (`agent_common.LEGACY_RATIFICATION`
# is the one home for the translation, and `bootstrap.py --migrate-config`
# applies it to a legacy `docs/gate-policy` file):
#
#   attended       human_ratification_through = 4, keep_nondependent = false
#   single-ratify  = 0, keep_nondependent = true, final_review = "always"
#   autonomous     = 0, keep_nondependent = true, final_review = "off"
#
# Note what that makes visible: the enum's words were never one axis. Each
# bundled a tier hold, a drain policy and an end-of-run hold, which is exactly
# why four separate tables had to re-interpret the same word.
# How far up the SN -> SR -> LLR -> TC spine a HUMAN ratifies, cumulatively
# ("through" this tier). The dispatcher compares the IN-PROCESS stage of a row
# against this number: at or below it, work SURFACES for the human; above it,
# the loop dispatches and an LLM verdict carries ratification authority.
#   0 = nothing is human-held; the loop ratifies every tier itself
#   1 = the human ratifies SNs
#   2 = ... and SRs
#   3 = ... and LLRs
#   4 = ... and TCs (every tier human-held; the most conservative setting)
# Named `human_ratification_through`, never "attest level": `Attest` is already
# a Verification method in the SR vocabulary and the collision would be
# permanent.
human_ratification_through = 4
# The separate "always show me the full breakdown at the end" hold, split out
# because it is flipped far more often than the level itself.
#   "always" — the loop stops for a final human read even when the level would
#              have let it close
#   "off"    — no final hold
final_review = "always"
# Keep NON-DEPENDENT work running while a ratification is queued? This is an
# ORTHOGONAL policy an ordinal cannot express (it was the old `single-ratify`
# level's distinguishing behaviour). false = a queued ratification drains the
# station and stops the run; true = other lanes keep going around it.
keep_nondependent = false
# How a CLEAN (`complete/`) lane close is adjudicated. `partial` and
# `cancelled` closes are ALWAYS adjudicated — they carry a claim about what was
# not delivered. A green close already passed the declared bar and the review
# rounds, so adjudicating every one of them prices a strong-tier session onto
# work two gates already judged.
#   "off"    — never (the review rounds are the judgement)
#   "sample" — spot-check at `complete_sample_rate`
#   "always" — adjudicate every clean close too
complete_review = "sample"
complete_sample_rate = 4

[policies]
# Who may publish (`git push`) this repo — was docs/push-policy.
#   "human"           the default: an agent never pushes, even if asked
#   "agent-iteration" an agent may push only the scrubbed llm/<branch>
#   "agent"           full delegation, still gated by the sync ritual
push = "human"
# How many independent fresh-context review verdicts a completed work item gets
# before the integrator accepts it — was docs/review-policy. 0 | 1 | 2.
review_rounds = 1
# Run the PII / identity-leak gate — was docs/privacy-check. The always-on
# secrets floor below is separate and runs regardless.
# KEYED-GREPPABLE: the three git hooks match this exact line shape so a
# Python-less box still fails closed (M-42).
privacy_check = false
# The always-on secrets floor (key/token shapes in the staged diff, message and
# outgoing range) — was docs/secrets-scan, where ABSENCE read as on.
secrets_scan = true
# The unwired-privacy-reviewer posture at the push boundary — was
# docs/privacy-review. Exactly "warn-unwired" opts the unwired case down to
# warn-and-proceed; any other value — including a typo — reads as require.
# KEYED-GREPPABLE, like privacy_check.
privacy_review = "require"
# Vendored-core guardrail injection — was docs/guardrails-policy.
#   "off" | "all" | "all except <sub> …" | "<sub> …"
guardrails = "off"
# The weekday (Mon–Fri) blackout window, UTC, HH:MM-HH:MM — was docs/blackout.
# The coordinator starts NO new session inside it; an in-flight session wraps
# normally, then the loop waits the window out and resumes. An empty value, or
# `start == end` as shipped below, DISABLES it.
#
# SHIPPED DISABLED, SHAPE KEPT (owner ruling 2026-08-11). This used to ship
# `"12:00-19:00"` — the kit author's own operating window — and every adopter
# inherited a business-hours blackout in a timezone they did not choose. The
# asymmetry decided it: a shipped-EMPTY dial an adopter forgets costs some
# odd-hours activity, while a shipped-POPULATED dial an adopter does not notice
# costs seven hours a day of a loop that looks broken. It cost exactly that
# once — it silently stopped ten of the kit's own tests for seven hours of
# every weekday until WI-428 caught it. `"12:00-12:00"` is inert at every hour
# of every day while still showing you what a window looks like, so filling it
# in is an edit rather than a lookup.
#
# WHAT TO PUT HERE. Pick the hours your own team wants the machine quiet, in
# UTC. If you want a starting point: the kit's author observes heavier
# contention roughly 12:00–19:00 UTC (= 08:00–15:00 US Eastern, 05:00–12:00 US
# Pacific) and runs that window. That is one person's operating observation,
# offered as such — it is NOT a measurement of any vendor's load, and nothing
# here should be read as one. Your own contention is the thing worth measuring.
#
# The window is half-open `[start, end)`: `12:00-19:00` blocks 12:00 through
# 18:59 and releases at 19:00. A start after its end wraps past midnight.
blackout = "12:00-12:00"

[checks]
# IS THIS CHECK ON. Six dials that used to be one-word files under docs/ and
# that no scaffold ever wrote, so ABSENCE was the declaration — which is what
# the owner overturned WI-423 over (2026-08-11): a repo that has decided
# nothing and a repo that never heard of the layer looked identical, and the
# only way to switch a check off was to CREATE a file. They are keys now, and
# every one of them ships VISIBLE so the decision is readable at a glance.
#
# WHAT THE OVERTURN DID NOT DO: change a default. Four of these are on, two are
# opt-in and stay opt-in — "key them all to on / true" makes the DECLARATION
# explicit, it does not arm a gate nobody asked for. A default reversed inside
# a re-homing is a prior ruling overturned without its own review.
#
# WHO READS THEM, and the cost that buys: `check_trajectory.py` (the first
# three), `gen_okf.py`, `subagent_gate.py`, `agent_loop.py`. The first three of
# those import nothing of the coordinator layer — F5 (owner 2026-07-12, no
# shared `_kitcommon.py`) keeps every kit script independently copy-able — so
# each carries its OWN small `tomllib` read of this section. That duplication
# is deliberate and BOUNDED BY A TEST: `tests/test_rule_sync.py` drives the
# copies against one table of file shapes and fails if they disagree (D-7, the
# anti-drift tool of record).
#
# LEGACY FALLBACK AND ITS EXPIRY. A checker that finds no key here still reads
# its old one-word file (`docs/trajectory-check`, …), exactly as
# `check_privacy.py` still reads `docs/privacy-check`. That is SN-028's ONE
# dual-read window, not a second clock: `bootstrap.py --migrate-config` folds
# every legacy file in and DELETES it, bootstrap runs it on every scaffold
# pass, and running with both homes live is refused rather than resolved by
# precedence. So an adopter's window is exactly one re-sync long, and it closes
# for everyone when SN-028's does.
# The work-item registry validator + its dashboard (check_trajectory.py,
# gen_trajectory.py). `false` short-circuits both before any validation. On by
# default and VACUOUS by default — a fresh scaffold's placeholder-only registry
# validates and renders nothing — so leaving it true costs a non-adopter zero.
trajectory_check = true
# The architecture-connectivity coverage warns (WI-056): every arch-map module
# is a declared IF-### endpoint, each Active seam is cited by a TC. All
# warn-first — they never change an exit code — and vacuous on a ≤1-module
# inventory. `false` silences the layer.
interfaces_check = true
# The How-SW top-view right-sizing bound and the knowledge⇒component coupling
# (WI-073 / WI-153). WARN at the plain run, ERROR under `--strict` (DevStg-Tests+);
# trivially passed below the 10-item bound. `false` silences both.
components_check = true
# The OKF knowledge-bundle export (gen_okf.py) and its freshness gate.
# `false` silences the layer; a placeholder-only registry emits nothing anyway.
okf_export = true
# OPT-IN, and shipped at its current default. The coordinator console's
# in-place one-line-per-workstream status render — `true` is the same request
# as `agent_loop.py --live-status`, and either way it applies only when stdout
# is a TTY with VT (a pipe or CI log keeps the append-only scroll). Off means
# the scroll, which is what every repo gets today.
live_status = false
# OPT-IN, and shipped at its current default. The subagent-spawn gate
# (subagent_gate.py, a Claude `PreToolUse` hook) — bounded fan-out for an
# unattended run. NOT a boolean, because it was never one:
#   "off"   allow every spawn (the default; the hook is a no-op)
#   "ask"   defer each spawn to approval
#   "deny"  refuse every spawn
# `ask`/`deny` is a real restriction on what an agent may do, so it stays a
# thing an adopter TURNS ON. Shipping it visibly at "off" is what the overturn
# asked for — the dial is now readable without arming it.
subagent_gate = "off"
# REVERSE BACK-LINK COVERAGE, as a NUMBER rather than a yes/no (OI-42 ruled
# (b)+(e), 2026-08-20). The minimum PERCENTAGE of your live LLR rows that must
# be named by a literal `Implements:` declaration somewhere under
# `docs/stack.ini` `[paths] src`. `0` is the off position — the number is
# reported on every run and nothing is gated — and it is what ships.
#
# WHY IT SHIPS AT 0 AND NOT AT THE TARGET. The convention this measures is
# stated in `process.md` and `AGENTS.template.md`, and the kit's own scripts
# followed it at 2 of 781 public symbols when it was measured: 0.6% of live LLR
# rows. Shipping the bar at the 50% target would fire on the FIRST RUN in the
# kit's own repo and in every fresh scaffold, failing a bar its own author
# misses by roughly 80x — which trains readers to silence the warning rather
# than to write the tags. So the measurement ships and the bar does not.
#
# 50 IS THE RECORDED TARGET, and the ONLY sanctioned way to reach it is to
# write the declarations and then raise this number to a bar the tree already
# clears. The number goes up because the tags landed; never because the bar
# came down. WHERE THE STEP RUNS, stated exactly (corrected 2026-08-20): the
# `backlink-coverage` step exists from DevStg-Tests on and NOT below it, so a
# plain run does not warn — it does not run the step at all. From DevStg-Tests
# the step reports, and `--strict-backlinks` (which `check.py` adds at that bar
# and above) turns a below-minimum reading into a failure. Born here, so unlike
# those six it has no legacy one-word file and cannot be double-declared.
#
# READ THE NUMBER WITH ITS ONE CAVEAT: the scan reads comment TEXT and applies no
# position restriction, so a spine id inside a string literal or a negated
# example counts as a carrier. Because the denominator is your live LLR count,
# that error can only RAISE the score — false passes, never false failures — so
# a number near your bar deserves a look at what is carrying it.
backlink_coverage_min = 0
